\chapter{BASIC Frontend Language}
This chapter describes the Dartmouth BASIC subset supported by the EightBol compiler's BASIC frontend. It covers every statement, function, operator, and control structure recognized by the lexer and parser, along with their inputs, outputs, processing, and side-effects.

This BASIC frontend is designed to compile BASIC source code to COBOL/EIGHTBOL intermediate representation, which is then further compiled to target assembly languages (6502, 65c02, Z80, etc.). The frontend provides a bridge between legacy BASIC programs and the modern EightBol compilation pipeline.

\section{Overview}

The BASIC frontend accepts a Dartmouth-style BASIC source and produces an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) that maps to EightBol's COBOL-like IR. The frontend handles:

\begin{itemize}
\item Line-numbered source with optional quoted labels
\item Variable assignment and expressions
\item Control flow structures (IF/THEN/ELSE, FOR/NEXT, WHILE, UNTIL, DO)
\item Subroutines via GOSUB/RETURN
\item Object-oriented method invocation via GOSUB ... ON object
\item Library calls via GOSUB ... IN LIBRARY
\item Service dispatch via GOSUB ... SERVICE
\item Class and method definitions
\item PRINT, INPUT, and DIALOGUE statements
\item Assembly entry points and file inclusion
\end{itemize}

\section{Line Number and Label Format}

Each BASIC line may optionally begin with a line number followed by an optional quoted label and a colon:

@example
100 "Start" LET X = 5
200 LOOP: FOR I = 1 TO 10
300 NEXT I
@end example

Line numbers generate @code{BASIC_Lnnnnnn} labels in the generated assembly. Quoted labels after the line number provide symbolic names for lines, also translated to assembly labels.

\section{Lexical Elements}
The BASIC frontend tokenizes source code into the following categories:

\subsection{Keywords}
Keywords are reserved words that have special meaning in BASIC. They are case-insensitive.

\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{ll}
Keyword & Token \\
\hline
REM & :REM \\
LET & :LET \\
FOR & :FOR \\
TO & :TO \\
STEP & :STEP \\
NEXT & :NEXT \\
IF & :IF \\
THEN & :THEN \\
ELSE & :ELSE \\
GOTO & :GOTO \\
GOSUB & :GOSUB \\
RETURN & :RETURN \\
LOG & :LOG \\
FAULT & :FAULT \\
STOP & :STOP \\
METHOD & :METHOD \\
ON & :ON \\
CLASS & :CLASS \\
PROCEDURE & :PROCEDURE \\
END & :END \\
WHILE & :WHILE \\
UNTIL & :UNTIL \\
DO & :DO \\
LIBRARY & :LIBRARY \\
AND & :AND \\
OR & :OR \\
NOT & :NOT \\
PRINT & :PRINT \\
INPUT & :INPUT \\
DIALOGUE & :DIALOGUE \\
SERVICE & :SERVICE \\
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Operators}
Operators are symbols that perform arithmetic, logical, or relational operations.

\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{ll}
Operator & Description \\
\hline
+ & Addition \\
- & Subtraction \\
* & Multiplication \\
/ & Division \\
= & Equality or assignment (context-dependent) \\
< & Less than \\
> & Greater than \\
<= & Less than or equal \\
>= & Greater than or equal \\
<> & Not equal \\
. & Dot (used in qualified names) \\
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Delimiters}
These symbols separate tokens or have syntactic significance.

\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{ll}
Delimiter & Purpose \\
\hline
( & Left parenthesis \\
) & Right parenthesis \\
, & Comma (argument separator) \\
: & Colon (statement separator or label delimiter) \\
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Literals}
Literals represent constant values.

\subsubsection{Numeric Literals}
Numeric literals support multiple formats:

\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{ll}
Format & Example \\
\hline
Decimal & @code{42}, @code{1000} \\
Hexadecimal (&H prefix) & @code{&HFF}, @code{&h10} \\
Octal (&O prefix) & @code{&O77}, @code{&o10} \\
Binary (&B prefix) & @code{&B1010}, @code{&b11} \\
Dword (&D prefix, 32-bit) & @code{&D4294967295} \\
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

All numeric literal formats are converted to their decimal equivalent in the token stream.

\subsubsection{String Literals}
String literals are enclosed in double quotes (@code{"}).

Example: @code{"Hello, World!"}.

\subsection{Identifiers}
Identifiers name variables, procedures, methods, classes, and labels. They must begin with a letter and may contain letters, digits, underscores (@code{_}), and dollar signs (@code{$}). All identifiers are automatically normalized to PascalCase format in the AST. For example, @code{my_variable} becomes @code{MyVariable}, and @code{myVariable} becomes @code{MyVariable}.

\subsection{Line Numbers and Labels}
Each BASIC line may optionally begin with a line number (integer) followed by an optional quoted label (string) and a colon. Example:
@example
100 "Start" LET X = 5
@end example
Line numbers are used to generate @code{BASIC_Lnnnnnn} labels in the generated assembly. Quoted labels provide symbolic names for lines.

\section{Statements}
Each statement performs a specific action. Statements may be separated by colons on the same line.

\subsection{REM}
The @code{REM} statement (remark) inserts a comment that is ignored by the compiler. It has no effect on program execution.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
REM This is a comment
100 REM Comment after line number
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
Any text following the @code{REM} keyword on the same line.

\subsubsection{Outputs}
None.

\subsubsection{Processing}
The lexer recognizes @code{REM} as a keyword and treats all text from @code{REM} to the end of the line as a comment. The parser skips these lines entirely during AST construction.

\subsubsection{Variants}
\begin{itemize}
\item Lines beginning with @code{REM} (case-insensitive)
\item Lines where the first three characters are @code{REM} followed by a space
\end{itemize}

Note: The apostrophe (@code{'}) and semicolon (@code{;}) are \textbf{not} recognized for inline comments; only the @code{REM} keyword is supported.

\subsection{LET}
The @code{LET} statement assigns the value of an expression to a variable.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
LET variable = expression
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
- @code{variable}: An identifier naming the target variable.
- @code{expression}: An arithmetic or logical expression whose value is computed.

\subsubsection{Outputs}
The variable @code{variable} is updated with the result of @code{expression}.

\subsubsection{Processing}
The parser produces a move node that copies the value of @code{expression} into the storage location of @code{variable}.

\subsubsection{Side-Effects}
The variable's previous value is overwritten.

\subsection{INPUT}
The @code{INPUT} statement reads values from the user into variables.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
INPUT var1
INPUT var1, var2, var3
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
\begin{itemize}
\item @code{var1, var2, ...}: Comma-separated list of variable identifiers
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Outputs}
The specified variables are updated with user input values.

\subsubsection{Processing}
The parser creates an @code{:input} AST node containing the list of variable identifiers. At runtime, the backend generates code to read values from standard input into each variable.

\subsubsection{Variants}
\begin{itemize}
\item Single variable: @code{INPUT X}
\item Multiple variables: @code{INPUT A, B, C}
\end{itemize}

\subsection{PRINT}
The @code{PRINT} statement outputs values and strings to the console.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
PRINT "Hello, World!"
PRINT X
PRINT "The value is: ", X
PRINT
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
\begin{itemize}
\item Zero or more expressions separated by commas
\item Expressions may be string literals, numeric literals, or variable references
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Outputs}
Values are written to standard output.

\subsubsection{Processing}
The parser creates a @code{:print} AST node with the list of expressions. Empty @code{PRINT} statements produce a newline.

\subsubsection{Variants}
\begin{itemize}
\item Print string literal: @code{PRINT "text"}
\item Print expression: @code{PRINT X + Y}
\item Print multiple items: @code{PRINT "Value: ", X}
\item Print blank line: @code{PRINT}
\end{itemize}

\subsection{DIALOGUE}
The @code{DIALOGUE} statement displays character dialogue in game contexts.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
DIALOGUE $CHARACTER "Text to display"
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
\begin{itemize}
\item @code{CHARACTER}: Identifier naming the speaking character
\item @code{text}: String literal containing the dialogue text
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Outputs}
Dialogue is displayed with character attribution.

\subsubsection{Processing}
The parser creates a @code{:dialogue} AST node with speaker and text properties. The backend formats output to include character name and dialogue text.

\subsubsection{Example}
@example
DIALOGUE $HERO "I found the treasure!"
DIALOGUE $GUARD "Halt! Who goes there?"
@end example

\subsection{IF...THEN...ELSE}
The @code{IF} statement conditionally executes one or two blocks of statements.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
IF condition THEN statement [ELSE statement]
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
- @code{condition}: An expression that evaluates to true (non-zero) or false (zero).
- @code{statement}: The statement to execute if @code{condition} is true.
- @code{else-stmt}: Optional statement to execute if @code{condition} is false.

\subsubsection{Outputs}
None directly; execution flow is altered.

\subsubsection{Processing}
The parser builds an @code{if-node} with the condition, the @code{THEN} branch, and the optional @code{ELSE} branch. At runtime, the condition is evaluated; if true, the @code{THEN} branch is executed; otherwise, the @code{ELSE} branch (if present) is executed.

\subsubsection{Side-Effects}
Any side-effects occur within the executed branch.

\subsection{FOR...TO...STEP...NEXT}
The @code{FOR} statement creates a loop that iterates over a range of values.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
FOR variable = start TO end [STEP step]
   statement
NEXT [variable]
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
\begin{itemize}
\item @code{variable}: Identifier that serves as the loop counter.
\item @code{start}: Initial value of the counter (expression).
\item @code{end}: Terminal value; loop continues while counter has not passed this value (expression).
\item @code{step}: Optional increment (default 1) added to @code{variable} each iteration (expression).
\item @code{statement}: Body of the loop.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Outputs}
The @code{variable} is updated each iteration.

\subsubsection{Processing}
The parser generates a @code{:perform} AST node representing a loop with:
\begin{itemize}
\item Varying variable: @code{variable}
\item Initial value: @code{start}
\item Increment: @code{step} (or 1 if omitted)
\item Termination condition: counter > @code{end} (for positive step) or counter < @code{end} (for negative step).
\end{itemize}

The FOR node uses a greater-than comparison for termination; see source for details on step sign handling.

\subsubsection{Variants}
@example
FOR I = 1 TO 10
   PRINT I
NEXT

FOR X = 0 TO 100 STEP 5
   LET Y = X * 2
NEXT X

FOR COUNT = 10 TO 1 STEP -1
   PRINT COUNT
NEXT
@end example

\subsubsection{Side-Effects}
The loop variable is modified after each iteration. The loop body executes zero or more times.

\subsection{NEXT}
The @code{NEXT} statement marks the end of a @code{FOR} loop. The loop variable is optional.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
NEXT
NEXT I
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
Optional @code{variable}: If present, should match the corresponding @code{FOR} variable (though the current implementation ignores it).

\subsubsection{Outputs}
None.

\subsubsection{Processing}
The parser produces a @code{:goback} AST node that jumps back to the loop header. The actual loop continuation logic resides in the @code{FOR} node.

\subsubsection{Variants}
\begin{itemize}
\item @code{NEXT} — Continue to nearest enclosing loop
\item @code{NEXT I} — Continue to loop with variable I (variable ignored in current implementation)
\end{itemize}

\section{GOTO}
The @code{GOTO} statement transfers control unconditionally to a line number or label.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
GOTO target
@end example
where @code{target} is either a line number (identifier) or a quoted label (string).

\subsubsection{Inputs}
\begin{itemize}
\item @code{target}: Line number or label to jump to.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Outputs}
None.

\subsubsection{Processing}
The parser produces a @code{:goto} AST node with the target. The backend resolves line numbers to @code{BASIC_Lnnnnnn} labels; quoted labels become symbolic labels.

\subsubsection{Variants}
@example
GOTO 100
GOTO "LoopStart"
IF X > 0 THEN GOTO 200
@end example

\section{GOSUB}
The @code{GOSUB} statement calls a subroutine, saving the return address for later @code{RETURN}.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
GOSUB target
@end example
where @code{target} can be:
\begin{itemize}
\item A line number (identifier)
\item A quoted label (string)
\item A string literal with @code{IN LIBRARY} (resident library call)
\item A string literal with @code{ON expression} (method invocation on an object)
\item A string literal with @code{SERVICE} (service-dispatch call)
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Inputs}
\begin{itemize}
\item @code{target}: Subroutine destination as described above.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Outputs}
None; a return address is pushed onto the call stack implicitly by the generated code.

\subsubsection{Processing}
Depending on the variant, the parser produces:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textit{Local label or identifier}: a @code{:perform} AST node with the target.
\item \textit{Library}: a @code{:call} AST node with library flag set.
\item \textit{Method}: an @code{:invoke} AST node with object and method name.
\item \textit{Service}: a @code{:call} AST node with service flag set.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Variants}
@example
GOSUB 1000
GOSUB "Initialize"
GOSUB "DrawScreen" IN LIBRARY
GOSUB "Update" ON PlayerObject
GOSUB "PlaySound" SERVICE
@end example

\section{RETURN}
The @code{RETURN} statement returns from the most recently called subroutine.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
RETURN
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
None.

\subsubsection{Outputs}
None.

\subsubsection{Processing}
The parser creates a @code{:goback} AST node that jumps to the address saved by the matching @code{GOSUB}.

\section{LOG FAULT}
The @code{LOG FAULT} statement logs a fault code for debugging.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
LOG FAULT "code"
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
\begin{itemize}
\item @code{code}: A string literal containing the fault code (typically four digits).
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Outputs}
None.

\subsubsection{Processing}
The parser builds a @code{:log-fault} AST node with the code.

\section{STOP}
The @code{STOP} statement halts program execution with an optional code.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
STOP "code"
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
\begin{itemize}
\item @code{code}: String literal containing a stop code.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Outputs}
None.

\subsubsection{Processing}
The parser creates a @code{:stop-run} AST node with the code.

\section{METHOD}
The @code{METHOD} statement defines a method within a class.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
METHOD "name" ON "class-name"
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
\begin{itemize}
\item @code{name}: Method name (string).
\item @code{class-name}: Name of the class to which the method belongs (string).
\end{itemize}

\section{PROCEDURE}
The @code{PROCEDURE} statement defines a standalone procedure.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
PROCEDURE "name"
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
\begin{itemize}
\item @code{name}: Procedure name (string).
\end{itemize}

\section{CLASS}
The @code{CLASS} statement defines a class.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
CLASS "name"
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
\begin{itemize}
\item @code{name}: Class name (string).
\end{itemize}

\section{END}
The @code{END} statement marks the end of the program.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
END
@end example

\section{INVOKE}
The @code{INVOKE} statement calls a method on an object using the OOPS dispatch mechanism.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
INVOKE object "method-name"
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
\begin{itemize}
\item @code{object}: An identifier referencing an object instance.
\item @code{"method-name"}: A string literal naming the method to invoke.
\end{itemize}

\section{CALL}
The @code{CALL} statement invokes a procedure or subroutine by name.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
CALL target
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
\begin{itemize}
\item @code{target}: Identifier naming the procedure to call.
\end{itemize}

\section{MOVE}
The @code{MOVE} statement copies the value of an expression into a variable.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
MOVE expression TO variable
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
\begin{itemize}
\item @code{expression}: Value to copy.
\item @code{variable}: Identifier naming the destination variable.
\end{itemize}

\section{SET}
The @code{SET} statement assigns the value of an expression to a variable (similar to @code{LET} but with different syntax).

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
SET variable TO expression
@end example

\section{ASSEMBLY ENTRY}
The @code{ASSEMBLY ENTRY} statement inserts an assembly language label into the generated output.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
ASSEMBLY ENTRY "label"
@end example

\section{COPY}
The @code{COPY} statement includes the contents of another source file.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
COPY "filename"
@end example

\section{Expression Statement}
A standalone expression (without an enclosing statement) is permitted; its value is computed but discarded unless it has side-effects.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
expression
@end example

